symbiont_marker_categorization.tsv: Claude categorization of proteins based on KO match
symbiont_marker_deeploc.csv: DeepLoc 2.1 output
symbiont_marker_proteins.faa: Fasta sequences of the relevant proteins

All protein sequences are NCBI reference protein sequences with good KO matches.

Below is Claude's summary of this work.

What the exercise did. We tested whether DeepLoc 2.1 can read the targeting
leaders of Symbiodiniaceae proteins by building a native control set with
compartment labels that do not depend on any localization predictor. We took
657 proteins across seven symbiont genomes whose compartment was fixed by KO
orthology (function-lock) — cytosolic ribosomal proteins, mitochondrial
respiratory-chain subunits, plastid Calvin-cycle and photosystem proteins,
secreted/ER/membrane/peroxisomal markers — and ran them through DeepLoc, then
compared DeepLoc's top-1 localization against the function-lock label. Overall
agreement was 60.7% with Cohen's κ = 0.474 (moderate; chance expectation
25.4%). The association was highly significant but strongly
compartment-specific, which is the actual result.

What we learned about DeepLoc on symbionts. Performance splits by compartment.
DeepLoc is reliable at the leaderless/secretory-cleaved end — cytosol 91%
recall, ER 100% (n=7) — and on the Signals axis it recovers a mitochondrial
transit peptide in only 43% of true mitochondrial proteins. It fails
systematically on plastid proteins: 94 of 137 function-locked plastid markers
were called Golgi apparatus, and the Signals axis almost never reported a
transit peptide for them, instead reporting a signal peptide or nothing. The
consistent interpretation is that DeepLoc reads the signal-peptide start of the
dinoflagellate bipartite (SP+transit) plastid leader as secretory-pathway entry
and does not recognize the plastid transit portion — expected for a model not
trained on peridinin-plastid targeting. Reading the confusion matrix by column
rather than row, the calibration is still usable: a Plastid call had 100%
precision and true mitochondrial proteins never landed in the Plastid or
Extracellular columns (0/143), so those calls carry specific meaning even
though recall is poor.

The baseline we do not have. This test was run only on Symbiodiniaceae. We did
not run the same function-lock control panel on coral hosts or any other
lineage, so we cannot separate "DeepLoc is bad at symbiont plastid leaders
specifically" from "DeepLoc has this error structure more broadly." DeepLoc's
published benchmarks are on curated, largely model-organism proteins and are
not a substitute for a same-style function-lock test on a comparison clade.
Concretely, the plastid→Golgi collapse is consistent with a
dinoflagellate-specific bipartite-leader confound, but without a coral-host (or
land-plant/green-algal) control run we cannot confirm it is lineage-specific
rather than a general DeepLoc weakness on plastid/bipartite leaders. The 60.7%
overall figure is therefore an internal calibration for these seven symbiont
genomes, not a claim about DeepLoc's relative accuracy across hosts. The clean
way to close that gap is to repeat this exact protocol — KO function-lock,
native controls, same seven-class panel — on a coral-host proteome and compare
the per-compartment recall and precision matrices.

Weakest link: the small control classes (ER n=7, secreted n=28, peroxisome
n=24) have wide intervals, so the headline numbers rest on the three large
classes (cytosol 293, mitochondrion 143, plastid 137). The baseline caveat
above is the dominant open question, and the validating experiment is the
coral-host replicate of this same test.
